  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises and their support systems to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, the government Agency for SME support (ODA) and other SME support organizations introduced the ILO&#x2019;s Start and Improve Your Business programme. The first phase of the programme had a specific focus on entrepreneurs in rural areas, particularly women, who could launch new green businesses or green their ongoing operations. Thanks to 25 SIYB-certified trainers, 1001 potential entrepreneurs, including 705 women, have gone through the program and have become eligible to apply for grants, including through ODA&#x2019;s own programme. In 2023, ODA and five Business Incubators launched the &apos;Support to Green Grants Challenge&apos; initiative, promoting eco-friendly practices for new and existing businesses. This programme offers grants and coaching to selected MSMEs. By November 2023, the program has trained and coached 200 rural entrepreneurs in green business, out of whom, 120 were supported with access to financing and business plans, provided development assistance to 50 new green start-ups, equipped five SMEs with green solutions, and practiced (including three women-led rural businesses), and funded three start-ups (including one women-led) for their green enterprises
In November 2023, the tripartite National Commission for Consultation and Collective Bargaining adopted a policy document on a Just Transition, which provides some insights into pathways to create an enabling environment for green entrepreneurship
 Through RBSA, the ILO supported the launch of the SIYB programme in Moldova. The ILO provided the Training of Trainers (ToT) and accompanied the SME support organizations during the pilot. 
The ILO supported ODA and five Business Incubators in rolling out the &#x2018;Support to Green Grants Challenge&#x2019; initiative. This initiative aimed to enhance access to public finances, particularly through the ODA-implemented ECO IMM Program. With ILO support, by November 2023, 200 rural entrepreneurs had been trained and coached in green business, out of whom, 120 were supported to access financing and develop business plans, provided development assistance to 50 new green start-ups, equipped five SMEs with green solutions, and practiced (including three women-led rural businesses), and funded three start-ups (including one women-led) for their green enterprises.
The ILO provided technical assistance to constituents in drafting a policy document on just transition. This was achieved through their exposure to capacity-building sessions held by ITC ILO. In August 2023, with technical guidance from ACTEMP, the Employers&apos; Organization developed an analysis of the challenges confronted by the private sector in transitioning to a green economy. The report was subsequently presented for discussion to the key stakeholders in September 2023</narrative>
   </description>
